WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.246
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online C two, Book three.

2
00:00:05.546 --> 00:00:09.164
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.464 --> 00:00:16.412
Unit five: Evolutionary Psychology. Today you will trace origins using trace back to / originate from.

4
00:00:16.712 --> 00:00:19.597
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:20.533 --> 00:00:24.138
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:24.983 --> 00:00:26.170
Adaptation.

7
00:00:27.183 --> 00:00:31.389
Adaptation means a trait that helps an organism survive and reproduce.

8
00:00:31.983 --> 00:00:34.227
Fear of snakes may be an adaptation.

9
00:00:34.817 --> 00:00:38.050
Your turn. Say: Adaptation.

10
00:00:40.900 --> 00:00:42.133
Adaptation.

11
00:00:42.883 --> 00:00:44.140
Natural selection.

12
00:00:45.150 --> 00:00:49.611
Natural selection means the process by which helpful traits become more common.

13
00:00:50.217 --> 00:00:53.238
Natural selection favours traits that raise survival.

14
00:00:53.850 --> 00:00:56.769
Your turn. Say: Natural selection.

15
00:00:59.633 --> 00:01:01.215
Natural selection.

16
00:01:01.950 --> 00:01:03.671
Evolutionary fitness.

17
00:01:04.683 --> 00:01:09.249
Evolutionary fitness means an organism's success in passing on its genes.

18
00:01:09.850 --> 00:01:13.707
Evolutionary fitness depends on reproduction, not strength.

19
00:01:14.317 --> 00:01:17.677
Your turn. Say: Evolutionary fitness.

20
00:01:20.533 --> 00:01:22.394
Evolutionary fitness.

21
00:01:23.150 --> 00:01:24.453
Mate selection.

22
00:01:25.450 --> 00:01:29.098
Mate selection means the process of choosing a partner for reproduction.

23
00:01:29.683 --> 00:01:32.612
Mate selection shapes many animal displays.

24
00:01:33.217 --> 00:01:35.939
Your turn. Say: Mate selection.

25
00:01:38.800 --> 00:01:39.929
Mate selection.

26
00:01:40.683 --> 00:01:41.893
Kin selection.

27
00:01:42.883 --> 00:01:46.717
Kin selection means favouring relatives because they share one's genes.

28
00:01:47.317 --> 00:01:50.791
Kin selection can explain why parents sacrifice for children.

29
00:01:51.383 --> 00:01:54.094
Your turn. Say: Kin selection.

30
00:01:56.933 --> 00:01:58.085
Kin selection.

31
00:01:58.850 --> 00:01:59.839
Instinct.

32
00:02:00.850 --> 00:02:04.521
Instinct means an inborn tendency to behave in a certain way.

33
00:02:05.117 --> 00:02:07.000
Newborns suck by instinct.

34
00:02:07.617 --> 00:02:10.130
Your turn. Say: Instinct.

35
00:02:12.967 --> 00:02:13.886
Instinct.

36
00:02:14.650 --> 00:02:15.988
Behavioral trait.

37
00:02:16.983 --> 00:02:19.981
Behavioral trait means a consistent pattern of behaviour.

38
00:02:20.583 --> 00:02:24.173
Curiosity is a behavioural trait found in many species.

39
00:02:24.783 --> 00:02:27.587
Your turn. Say: Behavioral trait.

40
00:02:30.433 --> 00:02:32.050
Behavioral trait.

41
00:02:32.783 --> 00:02:34.516
Ancestral environment.

42
00:02:35.517 --> 00:02:39.652
Ancestral environment means the conditions in which our ancestors evolved.

43
00:02:40.250 --> 00:02:43.097
Our taste for sugar suits the ancestral environment.

44
00:02:43.683 --> 00:02:47.497
Your turn. Say: Ancestral environment.

45
00:02:50.333 --> 00:02:52.054
Ancestral environment.

46
00:02:52.867 --> 00:02:56.564
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:57.417 --> 00:03:02.504
Visal, why do so many people fear spiders even if they have never met a dangerous one?

48
00:03:03.617 --> 00:03:11.108
Many instincts trace back to our ancestral environment, where such fears protected people from harm.

49
00:03:12.217 --> 00:03:13.821
So the fear is inherited?

50
00:03:14.917 --> 00:03:23.270
The disposition is. Natural selection favoured those who avoided danger, and the tendency originates from those survivors.

51
00:03:24.383 --> 00:03:26.104
Does that mean it is fixed?

52
00:03:27.217 --> 00:03:36.722
No. Culture and learning shape it. Evolutionary psychology claims only that some traits can be traced back to adaptation.

53
00:03:37.867 --> 00:03:39.425
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:40.283 --> 00:03:43.804
Grammar focus: Trace back to and originate from.

55
00:03:44.104 --> 00:03:45.233
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:45.917 --> 00:03:48.996
Many instincts trace back to our ancestral environment.

57
00:03:49.883 --> 00:03:52.649
The tendency originates from early survivors.

58
00:03:53.550 --> 00:03:56.269
Some traits can be traced back to adaptation.

59
00:03:57.233 --> 00:04:05.194
Trace back to, originate from, date back to, go back to + origin. Do not use 'originate in from'. Passive: can be traced back to.

60
00:04:05.950 --> 00:04:10.054
Be careful! Don't say: Instincts trace to back the past.

61
00:04:12.000 --> 00:04:14.743
Say: Instincts trace back to the past.

62
00:04:15.043 --> 00:04:16.555
trace back to + noun.

63
00:04:17.367 --> 00:04:20.193
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:21.000 --> 00:04:22.477
Echoes of the Past.

65
00:04:22.777 --> 00:04:27.238
Evolutionary psychology asks how natural selection shaped the human mind.

66
00:04:28.233 --> 00:04:34.668
Many instincts trace back to the ancestral environment, in which fear of snakes or heights raised the odds of survival.

67
00:04:35.667 --> 00:04:41.892
Kin selection explains why people often sacrifice for relatives, since helping kin spreads shared genes.

68
00:04:42.900 --> 00:04:48.893
Critics caution that explanations of this kind are hard to test and can be confused with stereotypes.

69
00:04:49.900 --> 00:04:55.592
Culture, learning and choice remain powerful, so an origin in adaptation is not a destiny.

70
00:04:56.700 --> 00:05:01.155
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:05:02.017 --> 00:05:05.514
Many instincts trace blank to our ancestral environment.

72
00:05:05.814 --> 00:05:08.643
A, back. B, up. C, off.

73
00:05:12.200 --> 00:05:13.933
The answer is A: back.

74
00:05:15.083 --> 00:05:18.337
A trait that helps survival and reproduction is an blank.

75
00:05:18.637 --> 00:05:23.277
A, adaptation. B, instinct only. C, environment.

76
00:05:26.833 --> 00:05:28.926
The answer is A: adaptation.

77
00:05:30.083 --> 00:05:32.977
Favouring relatives who share your genes is blank.

78
00:05:33.277 --> 00:05:37.151
A, mate selection. B, kin selection. C, fitness.

79
00:05:40.700 --> 00:05:43.025
The answer is B: kin selection.

80
00:05:44.183 --> 00:05:45.916
Choose the correct sentence.

81
00:05:46.216 --> 00:05:53.619
A, The fear originates from our past.. B, The fear originates of our past.. C, The fear originated by our past..

82
00:05:57.167 --> 00:06:00.525
The answer is A: The fear originates from our past..

83
00:06:01.683 --> 00:06:04.879
According to the reading, an origin in adaptation is…

84
00:06:05.179 --> 00:06:10.028
A, a destiny. B, not a destiny. C, a stereotype.

85
00:06:13.567 --> 00:06:16.135
The answer is B: not a destiny.

86
00:06:17.333 --> 00:06:20.218
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:21.083 --> 00:06:24.755
Can you think of a modern behavior that might have evolutionary roots?

88
00:06:41.550 --> 00:06:43.085
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:43.385 --> 00:07:05.222
Our craving for sugary and fatty food may have evolutionary roots. In the ancestral environment, calories were scarce, so a taste for them helped survival. Today food is abundant, so the same instinct contributes to obesity. The behaviour traces back to adaptation, but it is now mismatched with our surroundings.

90
00:07:05.522 --> 00:07:07.313
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:08.217 --> 00:07:12.657
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and trace back to and originate from.

92
00:07:12.957 --> 00:07:17.873
Today's value is scientific curiosity. Ask why, and then go and find out.

93
00:07:18.173 --> 00:07:21.046
See you in Unit six. Goodbye!

94
00:07:21.346 --> 00:07:23.871
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

